index 00000000..b03949c6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,200 @@
+# Two-node tebench RDMA sweep
+
+`tebench.sbatch` runs a target on one node and an initiator on another, using
+TENT RDMA with peer-to-peer metadata (no etcd or Store master). It discovers the
+endpoint from the target's startup output, waits up to 120 seconds, runs the
+sweep, and terminates its own target step when finished or interrupted.
+
+## Node selection
+
+The default allocation uses **two H100 nodes**: `slurm-h100-206-025` and
+`slurm-h100-206-035`, in partition `h100`, account `cw-sup`. The first node is
+the initiator; the second is the target. Each node reserves one GPU, eight CPUs
+and 64 GiB host RAM for up to two hours (including compilation). Busy nodes queue normally; the script
+does not touch existing jobs.
+
+Override the node list and partition using sbatch options before the script
+path. `INITIATOR_NODE` can select either allocated node to reverse the roles.
+The inspected cluster has only one H200 node, so it cannot currently provide an
+H200-to-H200 comparison.
+
+## Build and submit
+
+Submit directly from the Mooncake root; no CMake is needed on the login node:
+
+```bash
+sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+```
+
+The script builds on the CPU cores of the initiator node inside a writable
+Pyxis container. The base image is `ubuntu:22.04`; it binds the compute node's
+existing CUDA 13 toolkit into the container instead of downloading a large CUDA
+development image. It installs CMake, GCC and the required development packages
+inside the container, initializes the pybind11 submodule if missing, and builds
+only `tebench` with CUDA/TENT enabled. Store, EP, examples and tests are disabled.
+No host system packages are changed. First-time setup needs registry, Ubuntu
+package-mirror and GitHub access from the compute node.
+
+The compiled files go into shared `build/tebench-pyxis/`. The populated container
+is saved as `runtime.sqsh` there, and **both benchmark peers run in that image**
+so their libraries match the build. The launcher supplies the CUDA and TENT
+shared-library paths and binds `/dev/infiniband` directly. It disables this
+cluster's optional Mellanox remount hook, which fails on absent uverbs devices;
+GPU access still follows Slurm cgroups and `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`. Subsequent builds reuse that image and the
+CMake build tree. A lock protects the build directory throughout the benchmark;
+use separate `BUILD_DIR` values for concurrent independent jobs.
+
+To build first without reserving GPUs or a second node:
+
+```bash
+BUILD_ONLY=1 sbatch --nodes=1 --nodelist=slurm-h100-206-025 --gpus-per-node=0 \
+  mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+
+# After the build job completes, run the two-node benchmark without rebuilding.
+BUILD=0 sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+```
+
+This cluster exposes no CPU-only partition; the build-only command uses CPU
+resources on an H100 host and requests zero GPUs. The default combined job
+reserves its two benchmark GPUs for the whole allocation, including build time.
+
+Export an absolute `MOONCAKE_ROOT` when submitting from elsewhere. The checkout,
+build directory, saved image and results must be shared between nodes. Override
+`CUDA_HOST_PATH` if the toolkit lives elsewhere; `BUILD_IMAGE` can be another
+Ubuntu-compatible base or an existing squashfs image. A custom `TEBENCH_BIN`
+requires `BUILD=0` and a compatible `RUNTIME_IMAGE`.
+
+Examples (from the repository root):
+
+```bash
+# Default: H100 initiator, H100 target, GPU-memory RDMA.
+sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+
+# Reverse initiator and target on the same two nodes.
+INITIATOR_NODE=slurm-h100-206-035 sbatch \
+  mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+
+# Host-memory RDMA; allocation still reserves one GPU per node.
+SEG_TYPE=DRAM sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+
+# Longer write-only sweep: 1 KiB through 128 MiB, fixed batching/concurrency.
+MIN_BYTES=1024 MAX_BYTES=134217728 BUFFER_BYTES=1073741824 \
+BATCH_SIZE=4 THREADS=2 DURATION=10 OPS=write \
+  sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+
+# Short launch check: two sizes, one repetition, one second per size.
+MIN_BYTES=4096 MAX_BYTES=8192 REPEATS=1 DURATION=1 OPS=write \
+  sbatch mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
+```
+
+## Sweep and controls
+
+The default is **4 KiB, 8 KiB, ... 64 MiB** (15 message sizes), fixed batch size
+1 and one sending thread. Read and write sweeps each run three times; each size
+has five seconds of measurement plus tebench's warmup. Expected benchmark time
+is roughly nine minutes plus startup, excluding the build. No automatic batch/concurrency sweep is
+mixed into the message-size comparison.
+
+| Variable | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `SEG_TYPE` | `VRAM` | GPU memory; use `DRAM` for host-memory RDMA |
+| `MIN_BYTES`, `MAX_BYTES` | `4096`, `67108864` | Doubling sweep endpoints, in bytes |
+| `BUFFER_BYTES` | `268435456` | Registered buffer size; must cover maximum message × batch × threads |
+| `BATCH_SIZE`, `THREADS` | `1`, `1` | Fixed submission batch and concurrency |
+| `OPS` | `read write` | One or both operations, space separated |
+| `DURATION` | `5` | Measured seconds per size |
+| `REPEATS` | `3` | Repetitions of each operation's sweep |
+| `READY_TIMEOUT` | `120` | Target startup limit in seconds |
+| `INITIATOR_NODE` | First allocated node | Initiator host |
+| `BUILD`, `BUILD_ONLY` | `1`, `0` | Build before running; optionally exit after building |
+| `BUILD_DIR` | `$MOONCAKE_ROOT/build/tebench-pyxis` | Shared incremental build and container cache |
+| `BUILD_JOBS` | Allocated CPUs (8) | Compiler parallelism, no more than allocated CPUs |
+| `BUILD_IMAGE` | `ubuntu:22.04` | Initial Pyxis build image; cached runtime takes precedence |
+| `RUNTIME_IMAGE` | `$BUILD_DIR/runtime.sqsh` | Saved build environment used by both peers |
+| `CUDA_HOST_PATH`, `CUDA_HOME` | `/usr/local/cuda-13.0`, `/usr/local/cuda` | Host toolkit path and container mount destination |
+| `TEBENCH_BIN` | `$BUILD_DIR/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/tebench` | Executable |
+| `RESULT_ROOT` | `$MOONCAKE_ROOT/benchmark-results` | Shared result directory; do not use node-local `/tmp` |
+| `PYTHON` | `python3` | Standard-library-only result validation |
+
+`MAX_BYTES / MIN_BYTES` must be a power of two. Numeric controls are positive
+decimal integers, capped at 2^31-1 by the launcher. Increase Slurm's `--time`
+when expanding repetitions/durations. For controlled placement, configure the
+TENT engine with `MC_TENT_CONF` before submission; Slurm GPU visibility is
+preserved and each process uses its visible GPU 0. GPU RDMA requires working
+GPUDirect registration; the launcher reports failures instead of falling back
+to CPU memory or another transport.
+
+## Results
+
+A unique `benchmark-results/tebench-JOBID.XXXXXX/` directory contains:
+
+- `build.log` when compilation is enabled.
+- `target.log`, `endpoint.txt` and the exact target command.
+- `read-N.log` / `write-N.log`, matching command files and JSONL measurements.
+- Allocation, commit, role mapping, GPU/NIC topology, locked-memory limit and
+  per-node shared-library checks.
+
+Each JSONL file contains one record per size, including `block_size`,
+`aggregate_throughput_gbps` (decimal **GB/s**, despite the field spelling), and
+per-target mean/p99/p999 transfer latency. The launcher rejects missing sizes,
+empty measurements and failed initiator exits. Compare repeated runs rather
+than treating a single observation as peak fabric throughput.
+
+A read pulls target memory into the initiator; a write pushes initiator memory
+to the target. These are separate one-direction measurements, not simultaneous
+bidirectional bandwidth. With a single thread and batch size 1, small messages
+primarily expose latency and submission overhead; increase fixed batching or
+concurrency in a separate run to explore saturation. A file supplied through `MC_TENT_CONF` must be inside a mounted directory.
+GPU choice, NIC selection
+and other jobs on either host can affect results.
+
+## Validation performed
+
+- Job 1608 built `tebench` successfully in Pyxis on one H100 host using eight
+  CPUs and **zero GPUs**, in 4 minutes 13 seconds. It produced the executable
+  under `build/tebench-pyxis/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/tebench` and a
+  roughly 488 MiB `runtime.sqsh` image.
+- Job 1610 reused that image on both H100 nodes and completed GPU-memory RDMA
+  reads and writes at 4 KiB and 8 KiB, one repetition, one measured second per
+  size. Job status was `COMPLETED`, exit `0:0`; all four JSON records were
+  validated. The long-lived target step is intentionally cancelled at cleanup.
+  This smoke test validates the build/runtime path, not peak bandwidth or the
+  full default size sweep.
+- Logs for these validation jobs are in the shared workspace's
+  `mooncake-build-results/` directory.
+- Shell syntax and whitespace checks passed. Temporary stand-ins also verified
+  build-only success/failure, preservation of the old image after a failed
+  build, sweep completion, initiator exit-code propagation, startup timeout,
+  incomplete-result rejection and target cleanup.
+- `pre-commit` was unavailable in the environment.
+
+## Optional per-request event traces
+
+After rebuilding the modified TENT backend, use `TRACE=1` to record original
+request submissions and observed completions from both processes under the
+run's `events/` directory. For a short two-node H100 example:
+
+```bash
+export MOONCAKE_ROOT=/mnt/home/kimbo/networkx/Mooncake
+TRACE=1 REPEATS=1 DURATION=1 MIN_BYTES=1048576 MAX_BYTES=4194304 \
+  sbatch "$MOONCAKE_ROOT/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch"
+```
+
+The default `BUILD=1` rebuilds the binary first. An existing binary built before
+this recorder was added will not produce traces with `BUILD=0`.
+
+Each process/engine gets a unique JSONL file. The initiator log prints its trace
+filename, which associates the file with the operation and repeat. The target
+normally submits no requests for one-sided RDMA, so its file has no data events;
+the launcher terminates that waiting target and its final footer may be absent.
+Initiator files should end with `trace_end` and `dropped_records: 0`.
+
+Tracing also records warmup transfers. Unlike the tebench per-case summary,
+event files contain every observed request, and can be large. JSON serialization
+and tracing overhead can change measured bandwidth; retain untraced sweeps as
+the performance baseline. `TENT_TRACE_BUFFER_RECORDS` sets the bounded queue size
+(default 65536). Overflow drops trace records and is reported at shutdown.
+
+See [TENT event recorder](../../tent/TRANSFER_EVENTS.md) for the schema,
+completion semantics, clock interpretation, and use with an independent replay
+driver.
diff --git a/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch b/mooncake-transfer-engine/benchmark/slurm/tebench.sbatch
